Overall verdict

The structure is recognizable, but several governance details would cause a double take. The strongest concerns are inconsistent treatment of ex officio membership, an expired Compliance Committee seat, duplicate departmental representation, and an unusually powerful operational role for the Board Secretary.

Highest-confidence concerns

An expired member remains on the current roster
 The roster is dated April 13, 2026, but Bethany S. Ortiz, MD’s Compliance Committee term ended December 31, 2025. She should not appear as a current voting member unless reappointed or identified as a vacancy/holdover.

Ex officio status is treated as automatically voting
 The workbook is explicitly a voting-member roster, and all listed ex officio members appear as voting members. Ex officio describes how the seat is held, not whether it votes. Voting rights should come from the applicable bylaws or charter. The roster’s definition blurs those concepts.

The Credentials Committee has duplicate Anesthesiology representation
 Both Karl A. Wolfe, MD and Stephen R. Savage, MD are physician representatives for Anesthesiology. Meanwhile, several major departments have no representative. This could be intentional, but it looks more like population construction unless the charter authorizes multiple seats from one department.

The CMO chairs the Credentials Committee by virtue of office
 Daniel Gould, MD is CMO, voting chair of the Credentials Committee, and an ex officio voting MEC member. This is possible, but many organizations separate administrative medical leadership from the peer-led credentialing committee chair to support independent review and avoid concentrating recommendation authority.

The Board Secretary’s role remains unusually broad
 Kevin Caster is Board Secretary, Medical Staff Governance Coordinator, ex officio Board member, voting chair of the Compliance Committee, and a non-voting participant in the Credentials Committee. This combines corporate governance, operational committee support, Board membership, and compliance oversight in one position.

Additional plausibility concerns
The Governing Body “reports to” itself. The first row effectively lists Governing Body under both Committee Name and Reports To. A Board normally reports to corporate members where applicable, or the field would be listed as not applicable. The notes say directors are elected by corporate members, making the self-reporting entry especially awkward.
The Board Secretary is automatically a director. The notes say the Secretary sits on the Board by virtue of office. That is possible under governing documents, but corporate officer and director status are ordinarily distinct and should not be assumed merely from the title.
Nearly every MEC member is marked ex officio. Department chairs may hold MEC seats by office, but the elected medical-staff officers also have fixed three-year end dates despite being described as coterminous with office. The roster does not clearly distinguish the term of the underlying office from the committee seat.
Outside counsel is a voting Compliance Committee member. Adrienne R. McNulty, Esq. is identified as outside counsel of record and a designated voting member. Counsel commonly advises or attends, but formal voting membership may complicate counsel’s role and expectations around privilege.
No Privacy Officer, Security Officer, Internal Audit, Revenue Cycle, Quality, Nursing, or Operations representative appears on the Compliance Committee. For a hospital system, those omissions make the committee unusually narrow.
The Compliance Committee reports directly to the Governing Body, but its chair is also an ex officio Board member. This weakens separation between the committee preparing oversight information and the body receiving it.
Credentials Committee representation is highly specialty-specific but not facility-specific. The organization has two hospitals and an imaging subsidiary, yet there is no visible seat structure for campus or subsidiary representation.
Radiology has no Credentials Committee voting representative, despite the broader document set concentrating heavily on radiology credentialing and Winston integration.
The reservation-of-authority language is excessive for a membership roster. Restating the entire Credentials Committee to MEC to Board pathway and citing three controlling documents makes the spreadsheet read like a legal-position artifact rather than a normal committee roster.
Raw Excel serial dates are exposed. Values such as 45658 and 46752 represent dates but appear unformatted. This repeats the production-quality problem seen in other workbooks.
Internal inconsistencies
The notes say no more than half of designated Compliance Committee seats turn over in one year, but three of the five non-ex-officio seats end December 31, 2026. That exceeds half.
Bethany S. Ortiz, MD has an expired term but remains included in the April 2026 roster.
“Ex officio” is defined through tenure while the sheet uses it within a voting-member population, without separately documenting voting rights.
Governing Body reporting is listed inconsistently across rows, including self-reporting and “Members / Community.”
The roster says non-voting participants are intentionally omitted, yet spends substantial space defining them. That information would normally belong in the governing charter or a separate attendee section.
Strongest double-take items
An expired Compliance Committee member on a current roster.
Two Anesthesiology representatives and no Radiology representative on Credentials.
Ex officio status implicitly equated with voting status.
Outside counsel acting as a voting committee member.
The Board Secretary simultaneously serving as Board member and Compliance Committee chair.
The Governing Body shown as reporting to itself.
Compliance Committee staggering that does not match the stated rule.
Raw serial dates in a controlled governance roster.




